{"team":"DEN","col":"#FB6A28","identity":"balanced","funnel":"pass-funnel","plays_pg":61.7,"adot":7.3,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>No Fixed Address</h2><p>There's no bell-cow to bank on \u2014 the lead back sees just <strong>40%</strong> of carries (league ~59%), a committee that caps any one back's ceiling and makes their rushing props matchup- and script-dependent.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>DEN throw by design. They pass <strong>+1%</strong> over expectation (league -2%) and keep firing even with a lead (43% early-down pass when ahead vs ~41%) \u2014 the kind of pass-first identity that keeps receivers live in every script.</p><p>The tight end is barely used in the passing game (9% of targets vs ~15%) \u2014 fade their TE's receiving props.</p><p>Their backs are a real part of the passing game \u2014 RBs see <strong>21%</strong> of targets (vs ~18%), which props up <strong>J.K. Dobbins</strong>'s reception floor.</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they stay aggressive, throwing <strong>54%</strong> of the time, which puts scoring equity on <strong>Courtland Sutton</strong>.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 Stout Up Front, Soft in Coverage</h2><p>They surrender <strong>102 rushing</strong> and <strong>226 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). They wall the run and force the throw \u2014 <strong>attack with the opposing WR room</strong>.</p><p>A stingy, efficient defense \u2014 just 39% success allowed (vs ~44%); temper expectations for opposing skill players.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR2s \u2014 just 32 rec yds/game (vs ~36); fade receiving props there.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: <strong>opposing WR1/WR2</strong> are the target \u2014 this D forces the throw.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the current staff and roster set their own tendencies.</em></p></div>","depth":{"QB":[{"name":"Bo Nix","slot":"QB1","starter":true,"td":30,"props":{"Comp":21.37,"Att":3